---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PhysicsScenes from "../components/PhysicsScenes.astro";
import Toc from "../components/Toc.astro";
import { solver, paper, findings, figuresPaper, weyl, optics, site } from "../data/site";
---

<Base
  title="Research"
  description="Two-band superconductor simulation with a validated 3D TDGL solver, first-principles nonlinear optics of Weyl semimetals, and low-cost computational optics."
>
  <Toc items={[
    { id: "superconductivity", label: "Superconductivity" },
    { id: "validation", label: "Validation", sub: true },
    { id: "paper1", label: "Methods paper", sub: true },
    { id: "figures", label: "Figures", sub: true },
    { id: "paper2", label: "Second paper", sub: true },
    { id: "currently", label: "Currently", sub: true },
    { id: "weyl", label: "Weyl semimetals" },
    { id: "optics", label: "Lensmeter" },
  ]} />
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker">Research</p>
    <h1>Superconductivity, light and matter</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Simulation of two-band superconductors, the main line of work, and two
      projects started in 2026: first-principles nonlinear optics of Weyl
      semimetals and an open computational lensmeter.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">
      <a href="#superconductivity">Two-band superconductors</a> ·
      <a href="#weyl">Nonlinear optics of Weyl semimetals</a> ·
      <a href="#optics">Computational lensmeter</a>
    </p>

    <section id="superconductivity">
      <h2>Two-band superconductors: simulation and validation</h2>
      <p>{solver.summary}</p>
      <div class="versions">
        {
          solver.versions.map((v) => (
            <div>
              <code>{v.tag}</code>
              <span>{v.what}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <PhysicsScenes scenes={["diode", "vortex"]} />

      <h3 id="validation" style="margin-top:1.8rem">Validation</h3>
      <ul class="points">
        {solver.validation.map((v) => <li>{v}</li>)}
      </ul>

      <h3 id="paper1" style="margin-top:2rem">Methods paper</h3>
      <p class="paper-title"><a href={site.paperDoi}>{paper.title}</a></p>
      <p class="meta">{paper.meta}</p>
      <p class="cta-row">
        <a class="cta primary" href={site.paperDoi}>Read the preprint (Zenodo)</a>
        <a class="cta" href={site.solverRepo}>Code: juliaTDGL</a>
      </p>
      {
        findings.map((f) => (
          <section class="finding">
            <p class="kicker">{f.kicker}</p>
            <h3>{f.title}</h3>
            <ul class="points">
              {f.points.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </section>
        ))
      }

      <h3 id="figures" style="margin-top:2.2rem">Figures from the paper</h3>
      <div class="figs two">
        {
          figuresPaper.map((fig) => (
            <figure>
              <a href={fig.src} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                <img src={fig.src} alt={fig.alt} loading="lazy" />
              </a>
              <figcaption>{fig.caption}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <h3 id="paper2" style="margin-top:2rem">Second paper (manuscript under review)</h3>
      <p class="paper-title">Rectification in a notched two-band box at low field: two screening regimes and the terms that leave it unchanged</p>
      <p>
        At a field where the box holds no vortex at zero current, the voltage is
        carried by single σ-band vortices (or antivortices) nucleating at the tip
        of the notch, one per period; rectification is the asymmetry of the
        waiting time at the tip. Two screening regimes, and the effect of scalar
        potential, charge relaxation and heating on the onsets.
      </p>

      <h3 id="currently" style="margin-top:2rem">Currently</h3>
      <p>
        The same geometric diode at MgB₂-like parameters — strong screening and
        well-separated coherence lengths of the two bands — and what Joule
        heating and interband drag do to it. The longer-term direction is
        multiband, non-equilibrium TDGL for three-dimensional superconducting
        nanostructures, a regime that two-dimensional codes cannot reach.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="weyl">
      <p class="kicker" style="margin-top:3.2rem">{weyl.status}</p>
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Nonlinear optics of Weyl semimetals from first principles</h2>
      <p>{weyl.summary}</p>
      <p class="meta">{weyl.stack}</p>
      <PhysicsScenes scenes={["weyl"]} />
      <h3 style="margin-top:1.6rem">Completed so far</h3>
      <ul class="points">
        {weyl.done.map((d) => <li>{d}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Next:</strong> {weyl.next}</p>
    </section>

    <section id="optics">
      <p class="kicker" style="margin-top:3.2rem">{optics.status}</p>
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">{optics.title}</h2>
      <p>{optics.summary}</p>
      <PhysicsScenes scenes={["light"]} />
      <h3 style="margin-top:1.6rem">Completed so far</h3>
      <ul class="points">
        {optics.done.map((d) => <li>{d}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Next:</strong> {optics.next}</p>
    </section>

    <p class="callout">
      The animated panels are computed in the browser from analytic models (and
      from the measured diffraction data, in the case of the crystal); they
      illustrate the physics and are not output from the research codes.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
